{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789485529,"nonce":"iAZGGsED8CfWBuYqnyvrJP76a7Y=","title":"A closure in a static method reads a static field it just wrote as its old value","message":"```wren\nclass Pulse {\n  static start(n) {\n    __count = 0\n    Fiber.spawn {\n      for (i in 1..n) {\n        Fiber.sleep(1)\n        __count = __count + 1\n        System.print(\"tick %(__count)\")\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  static count() { __count }\n}\nPulse.start(5)\nfor (i in 1..30) Fiber.sleep(1)\nSystem.print(Pulse.count())\n```\n\nprints `tick 0` five times and then `1` in every mode (interpreter, jit,\ntiered), at 1d2f16f. wren_c prints `tick 1` .. `tick 5` and `5`: each\nread of `__count` in the closure sees the store before it.\n\nSo the store reaches the class's static (the final read from `count()`\nis 1, not 0), but every read inside the closure yields the value the\nstatic had when the closure was made, as if the reads went to a copy\ntaken at creation rather than to the class's slot. Follows 683f3dd,\nwhich made the closure find its class at all; a read-after-write in the\nclosure body is the case left. A `Fn.new` closure shows it the same as\na fiber body. Found writing caribou's frame-loop test; its fixture uses\na module variable instead.","files":null}]}